# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This project's own generated data + model artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IMPORTANT: the files matching these rules are ALREADY TRACKED in git --
# Data/dataset.sqlite, Data/OddsData.sqlite, Data/TeamData.sqlite and every
# file under Models/. They are deliberately NOT removed from the index here.
# The committed dataset is what makes the project runnable straight out of the
# box (`python main.py -xgb` works on a fresh clone); untracking it would
# break every existing user and is a product decision for the maintainer, not
# a cleanup a packaging fix should make unilaterally.
#
# These rules only stop FUTURE accidental commits. `.gitignore` has no effect
# on files already in the index, so nothing needs `git rm --cached` here --
# and it must not be run.
#
# They matter because the repository is already ~132 MB of tracked data on top
# of a ~434 MB .git: running the pipeline and then `git add -A` would commit
# another ~128 MB of freshly regenerated binaries.
#
# Note: the stock `db.sqlite3` line above is the Django convention and never
# matched this project, which uses `*.sqlite`. That is the pattern below.
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-journal
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
*.db

# Raw injury-report PDFs written by src/Process-Data/Get_Injury_Data.py: a
# backfill writes thousands of them and they are regenerable evidence, not
# source. The parsed view of them lives in Data/injury.sqlite (matched by the
# *.sqlite rule above).
Data/injury_raw/

# Serialized models / fitted artifacts
*.pkl
*.pickle
*.joblib
*.keras
*.h5
Models/XGBoost_Models/*.json

# Coverage output (htmlcov/, .coverage, coverage.xml are already ignored by the
# stock block above -- repeated here only to keep this section self-contained)
coverage.xml
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
.pytest_cache/

# Flask stuff:
instance/
.webassets-cache

